<exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-spanningsetforsubspaceofP2">
	<title>Finding a spanning set for a subspace of <m> P_2 </m> </title>

	<statement><p>  Show that
	</p>
	<p>
	    <m>W =</m> those polynomials with linear term equal to <m>0</m> </p><p>

	is a subspace of
	<m>
	    V=P_{2}
	</m>
	, then find a spanning set for
	<m>
	    W
	</m>
	.
	</p></statement>

	<solution>
<p>
	We apply the <xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test" text="title" />  to verify that
	<m>
	    W
	</m>
	is a subspace of
	<m>
	    P_{2}
	</m>
	.
	<ol marker="(i)">

		<li>
			<title><xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test-nonempty" text="title" /></title>

			<p>
			This collection is clearly not empty; in particular, the zero polynomial <m>0=0x^{2}+0x+0 </m>
			is in <m>W</m>.</p>

		</li>

		<li>
			<title><xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test-add-closed" text="title" /></title>
			<p>
		If <m>p_{1}</m> and <m>p_{2}</m> are both in <m>W</m>, then we can write <m>p_{1}(x)=a_{1}x^{2}+c_{1}</m> and <m>p_{2}(x)=a_{2}x^{2}+{c}_{2}</m>.</p> 
		<p>Then,</p> 
		<p> <md><mrow>
			p_{1}(x)+p_{2}(x)\amp =(a_{1}x^{2}+c_{1})+(a_{2}x^{2}+{c}_{2})</mrow><mrow>
			\amp =(a_{1}+a_{2})x^{2}+(c_{1}+c_{2}).</mrow></md></p>

		<p>
	Since
		<m>
		    p_{1}(x)+p_{2}(x)
		</m>
		also has a linear term equal to zero, we see that
		<m>
		    p_{1}(x)+p_{2}(x)
		</m>is also in<m>
		    W</m>.
			</p>
		</li>

		<li>
			<title><xref ref="procedure-subspaces-concepts-test-smul-closed" text="title" /></title>
			<p>
		If <m>p(x)</m> is in <m> W</m>, then <m>p(x)=ax^{2}+c</m>. For every arbitrary scalar <m>k</m>, we have <m>kp(x)=k(ax^{2}+c)=(ka)x^{2}+(kc)</m>, which also has a zero linear term, so <m>kp(x)</m> is also in <m>W</m>.
			</p>
		</li>

	</ol>

	Since <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>P_2</m>, it must have a spanning set. Any vector in <m>W</m> is of the form <m>p(x)=ax^2+c=a(x^2)+c(1)</m>, so the set <m>\{x^2,1\}</m> is a spanning set for <m>W</m>.
	</p>
	</solution>

</exercise>


<exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-analyzespan">
	<title>Analyzing a Span of vectors </title>

	<statement><p>
		Consider <m>\Span S</m> for <m>S = \{(1,2,4),(-1,2,4)\}</m>.
		<ol>
			<li><p> Determine if  <m>\uvec{v}=(5,-2,4)</m> is in <m>\Span S</m>.</p>
			</li>
			<li><p> Determine if  <m>\uvec{w}=(2,0,1)</m> is in  <m>\Span S</m>.</p>
            </li>
	<li>
		<p> Give a condition on the values of <m>a,b,c</m> so that <m>\uvec{u}=(a,b,c)</m> is in <m>\Span S</m>. Justify your answer.
			
		</p>
	</li>
		</ol>
	</p></statement>

