	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-spanningsetsubspaceofR4">
	<title>Finding a spanning set for a subspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m> </title>

	<statement><p>
		Let <m>V=\mathbb{R}^4</m>, and consider <m>U=</m> all vectors <m>(x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4)</m> where <m>x_1+x_2=x_4</m>, <m>2x_3-x_2=0</m>.
		<ol>
			<li><p> Show that <m>U</m> is a supspace of <m>\mathbb{R}^4</m>.</p>
			</li>
			<li><p> Find a spanning set for <m>U</m>. </p>
            </li>
		</ol>
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		
	</p></solution>

</exercise>



<exercise xml:id="exercise-subspaces-symmetricmatrices">
	<title>The subspace of symmetric matrices </title>

	<statement><p>
Consider the set <m>W</m> of symmetric matrices in the vector space <m>
    M_{n}(\mathbb{R})=M_{n\times n}(\mathbb{R})
</m>.
		<ol>
			<li> Show that for each <m>
			    n
			</m>,  <m>W</m> is a subspace of <m>
    M_{n}(\mathbb{R})</m>. </li>
			<li> Determine if the set <md>
			    S=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}\right\}
			</md> is a spanning set for <m>
			    W= 2\times2
			</m> symmetric matrices.
            </li>
	
		</ol>
	</p></statement>

	<solution>
		<p> 
		<ol>
			<li> 
				<p>
				We employ the Subspace Test: 
				<ol marker="(i)"> 
			<li><p> Since the <m>n\times n</m> zero matrix <m> \zerovec</m> satisfies <m>\utrans{\zerovec}=\zerovec</m>, the zero matrix is symmetric, so <m>W</m> is nonempty.
			</p> 
		</li>
			<li> <p> Let <m>A_{1}</m> and <m>A_{2}</m> be in <m>W</m>. Then <m>A_{1}</m> and <m>A_{2}</m> are both symmetric <m>n\times n</m> matrices, so <m>\utrans{A_1}=A_1</m> and <m>\utrans{A_2}=A_2</m>. We employ <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules-transpose-add" text="Transpose Addition Rule" />  to demonstrate that <m>A_{1}+A_{2}</m> is also symmetric:
<md>\utrans{(A_1+A_2)}=\utrans{A_1}+\utrans{A_2}=A_1+A_2</md>
</p></li>
<li>
	<p>
	Let <m>A</m> be in <m>W</m>, and let <m>k</m> be a scalar. Again, we know that <m>\utrans{A}=A</m>, so we use the <xref ref="proposition-matrix-ops-algebra-rules-transpose-smul" text="Transpose Scalar Multiplication Rule" /> to show that <m>kA</m> is also symmetric:
	<md>\utrans{(kA)}=k\utrans{A}=kA</md>
	</p>
</li>
 </ol></p>
				</li>
			<li><p> If <m>A</m> is a symmetric matrix in <m> M_{2}(\mathbb{R})</m>, then <m>A</m> must be of the form <m>A=\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\b &amp;d\end{bmatrix}</m> for some <m>a,b,d</m>. We can then decompose <m>A</m> as 
				<md>\begin{bmatrix}a &amp; b\\b &amp;d\end{bmatrix}=a\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}+b\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp;1\\1 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}+d\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}</md>, so <m>T=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp;1\\1 &amp;0\end{bmatrix},\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}\right\}</m> is a spanning set for <m>W</m>.</p>

				<p>However, this is  not the proposed spanning set <m>
			    S=\left\{\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}, \begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}\right\}
			</m>. We can use <xref ref="proposition-subspaces-eq-span-test" text="title" /> to determine if this is spanning set for <m>W</m>.</p>
		
		<p>	 Since the vectors in <m>S</m> are symmetric, every vector in <m>S</m> is a linear combination of the vectors in <m>T</m>, so now we need to check that every vector in <m>T</m> is a linear combination of the vectors in <m>S</m>. Starting with <m>\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}</m>, we note that 
<me>
\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}=0\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}-1\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}+0\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}.
</me> </p>

<p>
Now we tackle the less obvious linear combinations. We take <m>\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}</m> and build an arbitrary linear combination using <m> S</m>:



			
			<me> \begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}=k_1\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}+k_2\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}+k_3\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}k_1-k_2+4k_3 &amp; 2k_1+k_3\\2k_1+k_3 &amp;k_1+k_3\end{bmatrix}</me>

			Comparing entries on each side of the equation leads to the system of equations
			<md>
		\begin{sysofeqns}{rcrcrcr}
			    k_1  \amp  - \amp   k_2 \amp  + \amp 4k_3 \amp = \amp 0 \text{,} \\
			2k_1 \amp \amp \amp + \amp   k_3       \amp = \amp 1 \text{,} \\
			2k_1 \amp \amp \amp + \amp   k_3       \amp = \amp 1\text{,} \\
			k_1 \amp \amp \amp + \amp   k_3       \amp = \amp 0
		\end{sysofeqns}
	</md>
		</p> <p>
		Which we put into an augmented matrix and reduce:
		<md>
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
			 1 \amp  -1 \amp  4   \amp 0\\
			2 \amp  0\amp  1    \amp 1 \\
			 2 \amp  0 \amp 1  \amp 1 \\
			 1 \amp 0\amp  1  \amp 0
		\end{abmatrix}
		\qquad\rowredarrow\qquad
		\begin{abmatrix}{rrr|r}
			1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 1\\
			0 \amp 1 \amp 0 \amp   -3 \\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 1 \amp  -1\\
			0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 0
		\end{abmatrix}
	</md></p>
<p>
	The reduced matrix tells us that <m>k_1=1, k_2=-3, k_3=-1</m>. We can verify this:
<me> 1\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}-3\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}-1\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1+3-4 &amp; 2-1\\2-1 &amp;1-1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}</me>

</p>

<p>You can verify through a similar calculation that 

	<me> -1\begin{bmatrix}1 &amp; 2\\2 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}+7\begin{bmatrix}-1 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;0\end{bmatrix}+2\begin{bmatrix}4 &amp; 1\\1 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}1+3-4 &amp; 2-1\\2-1 &amp;1-1\end{bmatrix}=\begin{bmatrix}0 &amp; 0\\0 &amp;1\end{bmatrix}</me>

	Since every vector in <m>T</m> is a also linear combination of the vectors in <m>S</m>, this means they span the same space, so <m>S</m> is a spanning set for the <m>2\times 2</m> symmetric matrices.
</p>

            </li>
	
		</ol>		
	</p></solution>

</exercise>





</exercises>